-- title:
--   Inertia degrees are one over an algebraically closed constant field
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structure and assumed algebraically closed, and let $N, M$ be natural numbers with $M \neq 0$. For a level $n \neq 0$, write $F_n$ for the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the $q$-expansions $\mathrm{qExpand}\ \mathbb{Q}\ d\ \mathrm{jq}$ for the nonzero divisors $d \mid n$ (`modularFunctionFieldFull`), and write $F_n \otimes L$ for its base change `laurentBaseChange L`, namely the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the image of $F_n$ under the coefficientwise map induced by $\mathbb{Q} \to L$. Let $\varphi$ be an $L$-algebra homomorphism from $F_N \otimes L$ to $F_M \otimes L$ whose underlying ring homomorphism is integral, and let $W$ be a place of $F_M \otimes L$ over $L$, that is, a valuation subring of $F_M \otimes L$ which is not everything, contains the image of $L$, and is a principal ideal ring. Then the inertia degree of $W$ along $\varphi$ is $1$: giving $F_M \otimes L$ the $F_N \otimes L$-algebra structure coming from $\varphi$, the residue field of $W$ has degree $1$ over the residue field of the place of $F_N \otimes L$ obtained by restricting $W$. No nonvanishing hypothesis is imposed on $N$.
--
--   This records the standard fact that over an algebraically closed constant field no residue-field extension can occur at a place of a function field, here in the form needed for the degeneracy maps between the modular function fields of varying level. It is used in the analysis of places and divisors on the fibre models of modular curves, for instance in the computations of Hecke divisors and of the behaviour of specialised places.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_inertiaDegAlong_eq_one_laurentBaseChange.lean

